deep.reconstruction:
Setting: U/S is a relative open curve, X/S its smooth compactification with cusp divisor X \ U, and s ∈ S is a point of codimension 1. Let Õ be the henselization of O_{S,s}. It is explicitly the ordinary henselization, not the strict one. K̃ is its fraction field and S̃ = Spec Õ. For any base T, E_{U_T} → E_T denotes the arithmetic fundamental-group extension of π_1(T) by the geometric group π. The previous page built diagram (*)/(26). Its rows are E_{U_K̃} → E_{K̃} and E_{U_Õ} → E_{Õ}. The vertical kernels are the inertia groups I_s ≅ I_{s'} ≅ T, where T is the (tame) inertia ≅ Ẑ'(1).

This page first observes that, because the henselization is not strict, E_Õ ≅ Gal(k(s)^sep/k(s)) = E_{k(s)}. It also notes that specialization along U_s → U_Õ over s → Spec Õ identifies the bottom row of (26) with (27): 1 → π → E_{U_s} → E_{k(s)} → 1. This is the fundamental extension of the special fibre U_s over the residue field.

Summary ('Résumons'):
- A rational section f of U/S, or more generally a K̃-point f̃ of U_Õ, gives a Galois section E_f̃ of the middle row E_{U_K̃} → E_{K̃}.
- Composing with E_{U_K̃} → E_{U_Õ} ≅ E_{U_s} gives (28): a lift λ: E_K̃ → E_{U_Õ} ≅ E_{U_s} of the surjection E_K̃ → E_Õ ≅ E_{k(s)}.
- Here E_K̃ is the decomposition group of the local ring O_{S,s} inside Gal(K̄/K).

The following page states the equivalent conditions:
- λ is injective;
- λ restricted to inertia is k_i∘(μ·), where k_i: T → π is the cusp-inertia ('homo.-lacets') map at cusp i and μ ≥ 1;
- λ|I is nontrivial;
- the section does not come from a section of E_{U_Õ} → E_Õ;
- f is not regular at s;
- the closure of f in X meets X_s at a cusp i = f̃(s) ∈ X_s \ U_s.
In that case D_s embeds with index μ in the normalizer of the cusp inertia L_i.

Purpose: specialization of Galois sections attached to rational points at codimension-1 places. This is a step towards an induction for the section conjecture over higher-dimensional (finitely generated) bases. It distinguishes sections that specialize to genuine sections of the special fibre from those that degenerate into 'cuspidal' sections, i.e. sections landing in decomposition groups of cusps.
